  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 136 AM MDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nOne of my interests outside of meteorology is also something \ninvolved with looking at the sky. As I was driving into work this \nevening listening to classic rock (because I'm old), I heard a song \nthat fits with tonight, Harvest Moon by Neil Young, it looked\nbeautiful when I was driving in. Much of the area, not all but \na decent amount will be mostly clear through sunrise so I \nhighly suggest going out and taking a look if you can.\n\nOtherwise, things look relatively tranquil today. A weak cold front \nwill approaching from the north, and this will bring some breezy \nconditions to some locations. And, there could be just enough mid \nlevel moisture for an isolated shower mainly in and near the \nmountains. The chance is now more than 1 in 10 and chances were \nadded mainly for fire weather purposes. Most locations will see \nnothing and remain dry through at least Sunday morning. And it will \nbe a warm day, with high temperatures rising into the 80s across the \nwarmer spots like Worland and Thermopolis and possibly Casper. As \nfor potential fire weather issues, relative humidity should remain \nabove critical levels. However, with values dropping to around 20 \npercent, locally elevated fire weather is possible in the breezier \nlocations for a time this afternoon.\n\nThe next chance of rain moves in Sunday afternoon into Monday as \nsome moisture moves into the area, with an upper level low driving \nsome moisture from Hurricane Polo off of the Mexican coast.\nThis, along with some upper level divergence and the front \nbeing in the area. This does not look like a big rainfall event \nthough. This mornings model runs have come in somewhat drier, \nkeeping the bulk of the moisture to the south of Wyoming. Much \nof the southern and western portions of the area still have a \ngreater than 1 in 2 chance of over a tenth of an inch or rain, \nalthough this would fall over a greater than 1 day period. \nChances of greater than a quarter of an inch or rain greater \nthan 2 out of 5 are largely limited to the mountains and \npossibly the far southern portions of the area. And, we also \nhave to talk about a bit of snow. There is not a ton of cold air\nwith this system, with 700 millibar temperatures only falling \nto around 4 celsius, which would put snow levels around 10000 \nfeet. There will likely be some snow in the air. Accumulations \nwould be light though. The NBM ensemble shows the only area with\na greater than 1 in 2 chance of an inch or more to be in the \nWind River Range, largely above 11500 feet with no impacts to \ntravel corridors. Most of the showers should end by sunrise on \nTuesday.\n\nFollowing that, largely tranquil conditions should move back into \nthe area for most of the period as ridging builds over the northern \nRockies, That means another fairly lengthy period of above normal \ntemperatures and little chance of precipitation. We will will have \nto keep and eye to the south though. It looks quite wet down there \nas an upper level low moving from the California combines with the \nremnants of Hurricane Polo to bring some soaking rain and\npossible problems with flooding. Almost all guidance keeps this\nmoisture to the south and east of western and central Wyoming. \nHowever, upper level lows and tropical systems are fickle so we \nwill continue to watch this. Looking way out to next weekend, we\nmay really have a taste of fall as one of the first Canadian \ncold fronts may drop southward into Wyoming, bringing some much \ncooler temperatures. With the continental origin of the system, \nmoisture looks limited though. It is still a week out so a lot \ncan change, but we may finally have a decent taste of mid autumn\nSaturday or Sunday.",
